editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:227:"*Nothing to report.* I have written it every week for fifteen years. On the bottom of every return, in that hand." He did not look at the slip. "It is a true sentence. When there is nothing, that is what you write. It is the commonest line in the service."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:231:He had been kept by that sentence. He knew it as surely as he knew the ribs. Two years of true, empty weeks had gone up out of the wing with his name on the outside fold, and every one of them had said that nothing had happened. That sentence had stood between him and the people who read returns. And here was the same sentence, word for word, clipped to the back of a girl from a river town. In her case it said that the correction had held. Nothing to report. She had gone home smaller and stayed there, and nine weeks of somebody's careful watching had found nothing in her worth writing down, because the thing worth writing down had been taken off her paper before she was old enough to know it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:77:"A reissue is the lightest paper in any office," he said. "The words were settled before you were born. The clerk brings it in with the coal accounts and the lamp-oil order, and you sign where he points, because the last man signed there and the man before him." He looked at the yellow stack. "I have signed reissues, as every officer has. You sign them the way you sign for coal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:199:"She kept the books for a grain factor on a river quay, twenty years at the same desk with the same scale outside the window. I copied her ledgers from the time I could hold a pen, because the factor paid a boy a copper a page for the fair copies, and I was the boy." He looked at the stack of orders. "Every page of her ledger had a mistake on it, a weight misheard or a sack counted twice or a figure carried wrong. And every mistake was struck through with one line, so you could still read it, and initialled, and the right figure written beside it in the margin. That was what right looked like: a page that had been near something real, and been wrong, and been put right by a person who signed for it." He moved one hand toward the memoranda, the whole two centuries of them. "This has never once been wrong. There is not a struck figure in it, nor an initial in a margin. Two hundred years of paper made by people, and not one of them ever made a mistake." He took the hand back. "Nothing made by people is never wrong. It had been made to agree."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:47:"Then that's the rule," she said. "That's the one that matters. The rest are risks, and we can weigh them, but that one's a rule." She put both hands flat on the table. "He gets the case. Nothing about Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:227:"No. I am not with anyone."

