+| Keep reading | 8/10 | Chapter 35, lines 183–287, from the four-part unpacking of the case through “The honest people were in the drawer too” | Each layer of paper answers one question and opens a worse one. The father’s letter supplies a human victim before the institutional explanation becomes abstract. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 39, lines 203–217, “A conspiracy can be exposed. A convenience cannot” and “It isn’t broken. It’s maintained” | Making correct paperwork, ordinary competence, and inherited convenience the mechanism of harm feels specific and fresh. There is no easy wicked official for the reader to dismiss. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | Chapter 39, lines 65–165, Karis’s dating of the policy, the older reading, the old custom, and the entered limit | The logic is ultimately clear, especially because Karis distinguishes findings from limits. The density of “reading,” “function,” “entry,” “margin,” “policy,” “directive,” and “flag” can make this passage require rereading. | High |
